  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 181,
      "text": "MEXICO\n54. Recent labor market reforms should be complemented by measures to reduce\ninformality. The 2021 pension reform risks reducing formal labor supply incentives, including by\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 181,
      "end_char": 381,
      "text": "reducing the number of qualifying weeks for pension eligibility and raising contribution rates (Annex\nXIII). At the same time, the share of informal employment remains elevated and is associated with\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 381,
      "end_char": 575,
      "text": "low productivity. Recent reforms to reduce inequality—notably increases in the minimum wage—\nrisk increasing incentives for informality and reduce opportunities for workers to transition to the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 575,
      "end_char": 764,
      "text": "formal sector. It would be preferable, therefore, to align real minimum wage increases more closely\nwith average productivity growth of low wage workers. Other steps that could help reduce\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 764,
      "end_char": 958,
      "text": "informality could include: (i) continuing to improve labor dispute resolution mechanisms (building\non the experience of the 21 states that have already implemented the mechanism); (ii) lowering\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 958,
      "end_char": 1197,
      "text": "restrictions to layoffs; and (iii) reducing regulatory costs of formalizing a business. Also, improving\naccess to, and the quality of, childcare would increase female labor force participation.\nMinimum Wage and Full-time Wage Distribution\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1197,
      "end_char": 1433,
      "text": "(Monthly salary (40+hrs/week) andmonthly minimum salary, current pesos) Minimum Wage\n85 6,000\n120% OECD 25-75 percentile range, Pct of Mean Wage\nMin. Wage\n75 Mexico, pct of Mean Wage 5,000\n100% 2020\n2021 Mexico, real 2020 USD PPP (RHS)\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1433,
      "end_char": 1652,
      "text": "2022 65 4,000\n80% 2023\n2024\n60% 55 3,000\n40% 45 2,000\n20% 35 1,000\n0% 25 0\n2000 8000 32000\n2020 Formal 2021 Formal\n2022Q1-2 Formal 2020 Informal Sources: OECD and IMF staff calculations.\n2021 Informal 2022Q1-2 Informal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1652,
      "end_char": 1860,
      "text": "Sources: ENOE and IMFstaff calculations. Income and minimum wages are\nmonthly as recorded in ENOE exluding municipalities along the northern\nborder with the U.S. Minimum wages for 2023 and 2024 are estimated\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1860,
      "end_char": 1954,
      "text": "based on assumption of 50.4 percent cumulative growth.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 27\ntsaceroF"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 32",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 192,
      "text": "MEXICO\nInformality Rate in Mexico\n(Percent of total employment)\n61\n60\n59\n58\n57\n56\n55\n54\n2005 2006 2007 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022\nSource:INEGI.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 192,
      "end_char": 412,
      "text": "Note:Data on informality is missing for 2020Q2 due to the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on data collection.\n55. The USMCA trade agreement and its implementation remain important catalysts for\nhigher growth and reform.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 412,
      "end_char": 595,
      "text": "• The agreement with the U.S. and Canada reduces regulatory divergence, complementing the\nelimination of many tariffs under NAFTA. Implementation efforts include the establishment of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 595,
      "end_char": 753,
      "text": "new, independent labor adjudication courts at the state level.\n• An important challenge for Mexico is to increase the domestic value-added content of exports\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 753,
      "end_char": 944,
      "text": "to abide by the tighter rules of origin, including in the automotive and textile sectors. Meeting\nthese rules calls for complementary reforms, including through the structural fiscal reforms\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 944,
      "end_char": 1102,
      "text": "discussed earlier (e.g., to support education and training).\n• Mexico could also take steps to reduce trade barriers with other important partners, including\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1102,
      "end_char": 1257,
      "text": "China, the EU, and Latin America. Strengthening customs cooperation could further increase\ntrade integration with neighboring Central America (Annex XIV).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1257,
      "end_char": 1440,
      "text": "• The implementation agenda should be complemented with regulatory moves fostering more\ncompetition. High rates of firm creations suggest that the regulatory framework provides for a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1440,
      "end_char": 1632,
      "text": "low entry cost for businesses in many sectors. Yet regulatory hurdles in some sectors with critical\nforward linkages (e.g., foreign direct investment restrictions in surface, maritime and air\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1632,
      "end_char": 1808,
      "text": "transport) limit competition and, through the implications for input costs, harms the\ncompetitiveness of other sectors. Also, cumbersome licenses and permits procedures create\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1808,
      "end_char": 2001,
      "text": "vulnerabilities to corruption (e.g., road transport, custom brokerage and restaurants licenses,\nconstruction permits). Streamlining licensing and permit procedures would reduce vulnerabilities\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2001,
      "end_char": 2188,
      "text": "to corruption and improve competitiveness. Finally, the autonomous competition authority\nshould be strengthened by reversing recent budget cuts and preserving its statutory\nindependence.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2188,
      "end_char": 2362,
      "text": "56. Restoring a more predictable and independent energy policy would also boost\ncompetitiveness and investment. Actions to reverse the 2013 energy reforms remain incomplete,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2362,
      "end_char": 2484,
      "text": "as Parliament rejected the change in the constitution needed for a full reversal, which has\n28 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}
